{
  "request": "V1: accessible state space and classical cost",
  "status": "Conditional mathematical derivation plus synthetic executable validation; no new thrombin observations",
  "assumptions": [
    "There are M selected vibrational modes in a fixed bosonic basis. The initial system state is the vibrational vacuum, while nuclear bath trajectories sample the ground-state equilibrium ensemble. The bath is treated classically with no excitation-dependent feedback during propagation.",
    "The effective Hamiltonian is number conserving at every time. Its vacuum is the ground state and its scalar vacuum energy is subtracted consistently. Time variation does not introduce extra basis-derivative couplings in this fixed representation.",
    "The relevant dipole is linear in the selected creation and annihilation operators. Its coefficients may vary along the bath trajectory; a static dipole is not required. Permanent-dipole elastic terms are excluded from the target absorption band.",
    "Spectral comparison still requires physically validated trajectories, Hamiltonians, dipoles, ensemble convergence and optical/thermal prefactors. The deterministic test trajectory is not an equilibrium spectral prediction."
  ],
  "derivation": [
    "Define \\(N=\\sum_i a_i^\\dagger a_i\\) and \\(H(t)=E_0(t)I+\\sum_{ij}h_{ij}(t)a_i^\\dagger a_j+\\frac12\\sum_i\\chi_i(t)n_i(n_i-1)+\\sum_{i<j}\\chi_{ij}(t)n_i n_j\\), with \\(h=h^\\dagger\\). Then \\([H(t),N]=0\\) at every time. This remains true when \\([H(t),H(s)]\\ne0\\): the time-ordered propagator preserves each number sector.",
    "After subtracting \\(E_0(t)\\), \\(U(t)|0\\rangle=|0\\rangle\\). Write \\(\\mu_k(t)=\\sum_i[d_{ki}(t)a_i^\\dagger+d_{ki}^*(t)a_i]\\). Then \\(\\mu_k(0)|0\\rangle=\\sum_i d_{ki}(0)|i\\rangle\\), where \\(|i\\rangle=a_i^\\dagger|0\\rangle\\). Propagation stays in the single-excitation sector, whose dimension is \\(M\\). The ground plus single sector has dimension \\(M+1\\), potentially smaller after additional exact symmetries. A product basis with \\(d\\) local levels would have dimension \\(d^M\\), which need never be formed.",
    "All displayed quartic terms vanish on the vacuum and single sector. The exact projected dynamics within this conditional model are \\(i\\hbar\\dot c_k(t)=h(t)c_k(t)\\), \\(c_k(0)=d_k(0)\\). The trajectory correlation is \\(C_{kl}(t)=d_k(t)^\\dagger c_l(t)\\); average it over bath trajectories. Thus dynamical broadening and fluctuating dipoles survive this reduction. Isotropic absorption needs the three diagonal Cartesian correlations. No full propagator or eigenstate enumeration is necessary.",
    "Although a second dipole can create a two-quantum component from the evolved state, the final vacuum bra in the two-point correlation annihilates that component. There is no intervening propagation after that insertion in linear response. It therefore does not require a two-excitation propagation sector."
  ],
  "classical_cost": [
    "Let \\(R\\) count trajectory/time-origin samples, \\(K\\) resolved integration steps, \\(s\\) matrix-vector evaluations per step at specified accuracy and \\(z\\) nonzero entries of \\(h\\). For a fixed number of polarisations, propagation costs \\(O(RKsz)\\), hence \\(O(RKsM^2)\\) for a dense model. State storage is \\(O(M)\\); the instantaneous Hamiltonian costs \\(O(z)\\). Correlation accumulation requires \\(O(K)\\) storage if trajectories are streamed.",
    "An FFT on a uniform accumulated time grid costs \\(O(K\\log K)\\). Time step and \\(s\\) depend on spectral scale, integration accuracy and desired time window; this is not a precision-independent complexity assertion. Dense diagonalisation at every step would cost \\(O(M^3)\\) but is not required for vector propagation.",
    "Hamiltonian and dipole generation, conformational sampling and frequency-map validation have separate costs. No numerical wall-time or number of physical modes is inferred without those inputs. A four-complex cycle and model replicas multiply these costs, without restoring an exponential occupation-space factor.",
    "Encoding the ground plus single sector requires \\(\\lceil\\log_2(M+1)\\rceil\\) qubits, or \\(\\lceil\\log_2M\\rceil\\) for its excited block alone. This memory encoding does not eliminate Hamiltonian input, state preparation, measurements or trajectory multiplicity, and does not demonstrate a propagation advantage."
  ],
  "failure_conditions": [
    {
      "condition": "Thermally occupied system modes",
      "effect": "A thermal initial sector with \\(n\\) quanta has dimension \\(\\binom{M+n-1}{n}\\), and a linear dipole connects it to \\(n\\pm1\\). A vacuum-only calculation can miss hot bands. For a hypothetical independent harmonic mode at \\(1650\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\\) and \\(300\\,\\mathrm K\\), \\(q=\\exp[-hc\\tilde\\nu/(k_BT)]\\simeq3.66\\times10^{-4}\\) is the probability of any excitation. For \\(M\\) such independent modes, joint vacuum probability is \\((1-q)^M\\); small per-mode occupation is not a uniform large-system guarantee. This frequency is illustrative, not a supplied Gly216 measurement."
    },
    {
      "condition": "Anharmonic resonances changing total occupation",
      "effect": "Cubic terms such as \\(a_i^\\dagger a_j a_k+\\mathrm{h.c.}\\) can mix a fundamental with a combination band. They invalidate the single-sector model even with a linear dipole and an invariant vacuum. Their energetic relevance requires actual mode frequencies and couplings. The executable control proves only this algebraic possibility."
    },
    {
      "condition": "Nonlinear dipole or nonlinear optical response",
      "effect": "A quadratic dipole can create two quanta from vacuum. Repeated optical insertions in nonlinear spectroscopy can also access higher sectors. At a fixed excitation cutoff \\(n_{\\max}\\), the number of bosonic states is \\(\\binom{M+n_{\\max}}{n_{\\max}}\\), polynomial in \\(M\\) for fixed cutoff. Enlarging the sector does not itself prove exponential cost."
    },
    {
      "condition": "Counter-rotating terms, drive, or explicit quantum bath",
      "effect": "Pair creation/annihilation terms \\(a_i a_j+\\mathrm{h.c.}\\), linear forces, excitation exchange with explicit quantum bath modes, or excitation-dependent bath evolution can invalidate the stipulated vacuum/classical-bath construction. Pure quadratic Hamiltonians still obey closed linear Heisenberg equations for \\((a,a^\\dagger)\\), permitting polynomial classical propagation of correlations and covariances, including thermal states. Thermal occupation or number nonconservation alone is not a quantum-hardness argument."
    },
    {
      "condition": "Effects that do not invalidate the reduction",
      "effect": "Time-dependent number-conserving hopping, diagonal frequency fluctuations, classical bath-dependent linear dipoles, isotope shifts in the effective coefficients, and number-conserving Kerr terms all preserve the conditional sector argument. Generic use of the word anharmonicity is insufficient to demand a full product space."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": {
    "script": "ada/check_sectors.py",
    "results": "ada/sector-check-results.json",
    "method": "Full ladder-operator product-space action and independently written projected matrix-vector action, both RK4. Baseline includes noncommuting time-dependent hopping, local/cross Kerr terms and changing dipole coefficients. The cubic control suppresses hopping from mode 0 to modes 1 and 2 to maintain a bounded invariant weighted-occupation space; both control propagators use the same projected hopping.",
    "cutoff": "Full product space has \\(3^3=27\\) states. Baseline has one quantum. Control conserves \\(2n_0+n_1+n_2\\), initially at most two; occupations above two are inaccessible, so the local cutoff is sufficient, not merely assumed converged.",
    "limitations": "Agreement at floating precision tests the sector construction, while step halving checks discretisation error separately. No synthetic frequency, coupling or dipole is fitted to Q or P."
  },
  "source_locations": {
    "P": "Supplied publisher paper Sections 2, 4.2 and 6.2, printed pp7179,7181,7183-7184: classical bath/ground-state approximation and a fifteen-excitation plus ground-state example encoded in four qubits. ada/P-primary-recovery.json records primary prose recovery, not visual equation verification. New vibrational formulas above are independently derived, not transcriptions of damaged source equations.",
    "Q": "Supplied accepted manuscript pp18-21 motivates Gly216 and the 5F exception but supplies no vibrational Hamiltonian, quantum bath requirement, thermal vibrational populations or measured isotope-resolved spectrum. Source metadata and exposure remain unchanged."
  },
  "decision": "The specified linear ground-state amide-I pilot has an explicit polynomial classical propagation route and presents no established quantum-computing need. Replace the earlier unresolved accessible-Hilbert-space issue with this conditional result. Reconsider only if independently justified physical extensions defeat these reductions and an actual classical comparison shows a consequential bottleneck. P remains useful for its dynamical correlation viewpoint; a variational quantum implementation is optional algorithm research, not a necessary pilot step.",
  "remaining": "Whether this conditional model is physically adequate for Gly216; converged mode selection, frequencies, dipoles, realistic trajectories and independent spectra; force-field discrimination; experimental label feasibility. These are physical-validation gaps, not evidence of a quantum resource requirement."
}

